  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nHigh pressure settles into the Northern Plains tonight into \nWednesday morning, allowing cooler temperatures in the upper 20s to \nlow 40s overnight. Daytime highs Wednesday should be able to climb \ninto the 50s in most areas. Slightly warmer air returns heading into \nThursday as a cold front approaches the area. Nearly zonal h5 flow \nprevails ahead of the front, with upper flow turning northwest, then \nnorth by Thursday morning as the front dives southeast. A surge of \ncooler air brings overnight lows into the upper 20s to low 30s \nThursday night and Friday. Shortwave ridging then builds into the \nNorthern Plains for Saturday and Sunday. High temperatures both days \nare expected to return to above average values in the mid 60s to \nnear 70 degrees.\n\n\n...Freezing Temperatures Tonight into Wednesday Morning...\n\nCloud cover will slowly clear from west to east overnight, allowing \nefficient radiative cooling as high pressure settles into the area. \nWith sufficient clearing, temperatures should be able to fall into \nthe upper 20s to near 30 degrees; however, there is still some \nuncertainty about how much clearing we will see. If clouds are \nslower to clear, temperatures will run warm enough to stay above \nfreezing.\n\n...Enhanced Fire Weather Possible Friday...\n\nBreezy northwest winds are expected Thursday afternoon and evening \nas dry air moves into the area. Heading into Friday, RH values fall \ninto the 25-35 percent range, with winds diminishing heading into \nthe afternoon. There could be a brief period of combined winds and \nlow RH values, although supporting scenarios are fewer today than \nyesterday. Will need to continue monitoring the timing of the front \nas current guidance has added substantial forward speed, thus \nslightly reducing the fire risk.\n\n...Temperatures Near Freezing Thursday and Friday Night...\n\nCold air advection follows the Thursday/Friday system, bringing \ntemperatures into the 20s and low 30s Thursday and Friday nights. We \nare still watching for a scenario with more cloud cover, which \nserves to maintain a slightly higher overnight low temp. It is also \nworth noting that winds remain somewhat elevated overnight, which \ncould also play a role in keeping temperatures a bit warmer.",
